Looking at your memory manager code, here are several suggestions for improvement:

Free-list allocation inefficiency: Your flist_get_next_free_page() currently scans from the head every time and restarts the search when it encounters a gap—this results in O(n²) worst-case behavior for fragmented memory because you re-traverse from the found position each time but don't cache the search state. A better approach is to maintain a separate cursor or use a first-fit with remembered position (next-fit), or implement a segregated free-list by page-run size to reduce search time. Also, you're decrementing numpages inside the while loop but then resetting it to num_pages on a gap, which works but is slightly confusing—consider using a pages_found counter instead.

Memory poisoning granularity: You allocate a full PAGE_SIZE for poison data per chunk (overwriting data_size), but your poison check only verifies the first 64 bytes (via MEMORY_POISON_SIZE), leaving the rest of that page unused and unverified. You should either use the full page for corruption detection (check all poison_data_size bytes) or reduce poison_data_size to match what you actually validate (64 bytes) to save memory.

Alignment handling: You pass alignment to aligned_alloc but then assert total_size % alignment == 0, which is redundant since aligned_alloc only requires alignment be a power-of-two and size be a multiple of alignment—your assertion is correct but unnecessary, and you don't verify that num_pages * PAGE_SIZE is actually aligned to the requested alignment (e.g., if alignment > PAGE_SIZE, your total_size might be aligned but the start of usable data might not be page-aligned relative to the chunk start). Consider storing the offset needed to align the usable region.

Metadata storage: You store flist (the free-list head) inside the mem_block struct, which is fine, but your free-list nodes are embedded directly in the user memory pages, meaning you overwrite the first few bytes of each free page with struct flist (just a next pointer) and color bytes. This works but means you can never allocate the first sizeof(struct flist) + 4 bytes of a free page—that's acceptable overhead, but it's not documented and could confuse users if they expect full page utilization.

Table growth: Your mem_table is allocated with a fixed capacity of 16 chunks, but you don't enforce that limit anywhere—if you call mem_allocate_block() more than 16 times, you'll overflow the table. You either need bounds checking or dynamic reallocation of the table itself.

mem_deallocate_block() is empty: If you never plan to destroy chunks, that's fine, but you should either implement it or remove the function from the interface to avoid accidental calls.

Handle type sizes: Using int16 for memid_t and pageid_t limits you to 32767 chunks and 32767 pages per chunk—given PAGE_SIZE=1024, a chunk with 32767 pages is ~32MB, which is reasonable, but if you ever need larger chunks, you'll overflow. Consider using uint32 for safety unless you're memory-constrained.

Error handling: You call my_exit() on fatal errors, which is appropriate for your personal/educational use, but you might want a non-fatal error path for production scenarios (e.g., return NULL handles with error codes).

Redundant address calculations: In mem_page_release(), you call calc_address_from_page_id() multiple times per page, which involves division/modulo operations—for large releases this can be costly. You could instead iterate by pointer addition (cur = (char*)cur + PAGE_SIZE) and only compute page IDs for comparisons when needed.

Free-list continuity debug: Your debug_flist_check_continuity() is helpful, but you don't expose it in your public interface—consider making it callable from tests.

Code organization: Your internal functions are mixed with debug functions in the same file; consider separating debug helpers into a separate compilation unit or wrapping them in #ifdef DEBUG to keep the production code lean.

Overall, your implementation is clean and functional for fixed-size, known-memory projects. The main areas to address are the O(n²) free-list search (which becomes noticeable with many allocations/frees), the table capacity overflow, and documenting the page metadata overhead. Do you plan to support multi-threaded access, or is this strictly single-threaded per project? That would influence whether you need locking at the manager level.